1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,510
Welcome back. Earlier,
you learned how

2
00:00:03,510 --> 00:00:05,070
your approach can impact

3
00:00:05,070 --> 00:00:07,275
your ability to influence others.

4
00:00:07,275 --> 00:00:10,920
But influencing is about more
than just your approach.

5
00:00:10,920 --> 00:00:13,605
In fact, so much of your
ability to influence

6
00:00:13,605 --> 00:00:16,620
others comes from your
own sources of power.

7
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:18,450
Throughout your career, you'll

8
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:20,175
rely on your sources of power to

9
00:00:20,175 --> 00:00:21,450
influence others to do

10
00:00:21,450 --> 00:00:24,030
great work in service
of the project goals.

11
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:25,950
In his book, The Elements of

12
00:00:25,950 --> 00:00:28,725
Power: Lessons On
Leadership and Influence,

13
00:00:28,725 --> 00:00:30,480
the researcher Terry Bacon

14
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:32,910
determined that most
power sources fall into

15
00:00:32,910 --> 00:00:36,090
two buckets: organizational
and personal.

16
00:00:36,090 --> 00:00:37,875
From these power sources,

17
00:00:37,875 --> 00:00:39,480
we picked out the
ones we feel are

18
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:41,460
most important to cover here.

19
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:44,300
Let's start with organizational
sources of power,

20
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:45,725
which include your role,

21
00:00:45,725 --> 00:00:49,270
information, network,
and reputation.

22
00:00:49,270 --> 00:00:51,170
First we have your role,

23
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:52,865
which refers to your position

24
00:00:52,865 --> 00:00:55,310
within an organization or team.

25
00:00:55,310 --> 00:00:57,155
In a project management role,

26
00:00:57,155 --> 00:00:59,735
you have a certain level
of power over the project,

27
00:00:59,735 --> 00:01:01,970
and therefore more influence.

28
00:01:01,970 --> 00:01:05,315
Information is another source
of organizational power,

29
00:01:05,315 --> 00:01:07,415
which refers to your
level of access

30
00:01:07,415 --> 00:01:09,470
and control over information.

31
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:11,075
As a project manager,

32
00:01:11,075 --> 00:01:14,360
you possess a huge amount of
information about your work

33
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:17,015
and your stakeholders, which
can work to your benefit.

34
00:01:17,015 --> 00:01:20,075
Now it may be tempting to
assume that as a leader

35
00:01:20,075 --> 00:01:21,880
you have all of the answers,

36
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:23,660
but to be a good influencer

37
00:01:23,660 --> 00:01:24,980
you should ask questions

38
00:01:24,980 --> 00:01:26,885
and consider all ideas.

39
00:01:26,885 --> 00:01:29,000
You may even hear new insights

40
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,235
and notice issues
in a different way.

41
00:01:31,235 --> 00:01:34,100
Share information with
your team regularly so

42
00:01:34,100 --> 00:01:37,070
that they can provide
input on the full picture.

43
00:01:37,070 --> 00:01:39,725
You can also derive
power from your network.

44
00:01:39,725 --> 00:01:41,870
This refers to people
you're connected

45
00:01:41,870 --> 00:01:45,070
with professionally
and personally.

46
00:01:45,070 --> 00:01:48,020
Strong connections within
your organization and

47
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:51,170
beyond it can boost your
ability to influence others

48
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:53,710
and help your
project continue on.

49
00:01:53,710 --> 00:01:56,145
If you demonstrate that
you're well-connected and

50
00:01:56,145 --> 00:01:58,610
have a good chance of getting
stakeholders support

51
00:01:58,610 --> 00:02:00,440
or budget approval
for your plans,

52
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:01,970
then you're more likely to get

53
00:02:01,970 --> 00:02:04,070
your audience on your side.

54
00:02:04,070 --> 00:02:05,990
You should use your
network to help

55
00:02:05,990 --> 00:02:07,775
colleagues make new connections.

56
00:02:07,775 --> 00:02:10,790
Your team's effectiveness
will increase as you take on

57
00:02:10,790 --> 00:02:12,980
the responsibility of
helping each person

58
00:02:12,980 --> 00:02:15,815
develop their skills and
expand their network,

59
00:02:15,815 --> 00:02:19,150
which in turn increases
their own influencing power.

60
00:02:19,150 --> 00:02:21,185
Lastly, we have reputation,

61
00:02:21,185 --> 00:02:24,565
which refers to how others
perceive you overall.

62
00:02:24,565 --> 00:02:26,135
As you might expect,

63
00:02:26,135 --> 00:02:29,330
people with positive
reputations tend to be more

64
00:02:29,330 --> 00:02:32,920
influential than those
with negative reputations.

65
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:34,820
One way I've noticed this play

66
00:02:34,820 --> 00:02:36,580
out is through collaboration.

67
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:38,285
If you're a good collaborator,

68
00:02:38,285 --> 00:02:40,430
you can boost your reputation by

69
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:42,755
becoming known for being
easy to work with,

70
00:02:42,755 --> 00:02:44,210
keeping your commitments, and

71
00:02:44,210 --> 00:02:46,435
helping others achieve
their own goals.

72
00:02:46,435 --> 00:02:50,530
Your role, access to and control
of information, network,

73
00:02:50,530 --> 00:02:53,660
and reputation are important
organizational sources

74
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:55,190
of power to be aware of.

75
00:02:55,190 --> 00:02:57,200
It's also important to identify

76
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:00,005
power sources that come from you.

77
00:03:00,005 --> 00:03:03,320
These are referred to as
personal sources of power,

78
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:04,815
which include knowledge,

79
00:03:04,815 --> 00:03:07,940
expressiveness,
history, and character.

80
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:09,740
I like to think of these as

81
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:11,750
your own personal superpowers.

82
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:14,090
Knowledge refers to the
power that you draw

83
00:03:14,090 --> 00:03:16,625
from your expertise
in certain subjects,

84
00:03:16,625 --> 00:03:19,325
your unique abilities
and skill sets,

85
00:03:19,325 --> 00:03:22,010
and even your ability
to learn new things.

86
00:03:22,010 --> 00:03:23,630
What comes to mind when you think

87
00:03:23,630 --> 00:03:25,450
about an influential leader?

88
00:03:25,450 --> 00:03:27,620
Typically, they have
a lot of knowledge in

89
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:29,210
their field and you're more

90
00:03:29,210 --> 00:03:31,330
likely to trust their opinion.

91
00:03:31,330 --> 00:03:33,320
When you're
considering how to use

92
00:03:33,320 --> 00:03:35,555
your own knowledge as
a source of power,

93
00:03:35,555 --> 00:03:38,600
reflect on the expertise
you already have,

94
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:40,100
and consider areas where you

95
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:42,280
still want to grow
and learn more.

96
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:44,900
Expressiveness refers
to your ability

97
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:46,835
to communicate with others.

98
00:03:46,835 --> 00:03:48,305
This is a big one.

99
00:03:48,305 --> 00:03:49,520
As you learned earlier,

100
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:51,440
so much of influencing comes

101
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:53,930
down to how you
approach your audience.

102
00:03:53,930 --> 00:03:55,880
Maybe you're a great storyteller,

103
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:57,380
or maybe you're really good at

104
00:03:57,380 --> 00:03:59,315
connecting emotionally
with people.

105
00:03:59,315 --> 00:04:02,030
Strong communication
skills like these can

106
00:04:02,030 --> 00:04:05,830
significantly increase your
ability to influence others.

107
00:04:05,830 --> 00:04:09,485
The next personal source
of power is history.

108
00:04:09,485 --> 00:04:12,845
History refers to the level
of personal history there

109
00:04:12,845 --> 00:04:16,110
is between yourself and
another person. Makes sense, right?

110
00:04:16,110 --> 00:04:18,200
A strong relationship with

111
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:21,185
the person you hope to
influence can go a long way.

112
00:04:21,185 --> 00:04:22,715
As a project manager,

113
00:04:22,715 --> 00:04:24,350
you'll be managing team dynamics

114
00:04:24,350 --> 00:04:26,075
and building team relationships,

115
00:04:26,075 --> 00:04:27,350
which can help you build up

116
00:04:27,350 --> 00:04:29,270
personal history with
the people around

117
00:04:29,270 --> 00:04:32,780
you. And finally, character.

118
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:35,675
Character refers to
other people's view

119
00:04:35,675 --> 00:04:37,595
of the qualities that make you,

120
00:04:37,595 --> 00:04:40,400
you. Like, honesty, integrity,

121
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:42,395
kindness, and so much more.

122
00:04:42,395 --> 00:04:44,630
Character is a huge one.

123
00:04:44,630 --> 00:04:46,940
You'll derive an
enormous amount of

124
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:49,925
power from high estimates
of your character.

125
00:04:49,925 --> 00:04:51,770
What personal characteristics do

126
00:04:51,770 --> 00:04:53,510
you value within yourself?

127
00:04:53,510 --> 00:04:55,280
These are the qualities that have

128
00:04:55,280 --> 00:04:56,765
the power to make you a strong,

129
00:04:56,765 --> 00:04:58,955
supportive leader for
the people around you.

130
00:04:58,955 --> 00:05:02,310
Knowledge, expressiveness,
history, and character

131
00:05:02,310 --> 00:05:04,930
are just four personal
sources of power,

132
00:05:04,930 --> 00:05:06,890
but it's important
to remember that

133
00:05:06,890 --> 00:05:09,425
there's so much wrapped
in each one of these.

134
00:05:09,425 --> 00:05:11,240
There's trustworthiness,

135
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:14,690
authenticity, wisdom,
and on and on.

136
00:05:14,690 --> 00:05:17,570
There's so many attributes
to draw power from.

137
00:05:17,570 --> 00:05:19,400
I like to think of these elements

138
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:21,160
as personal superpowers.

139
00:05:21,160 --> 00:05:23,450
I learned early on
in my career that

140
00:05:23,450 --> 00:05:25,415
my personal superpower
is knowledge,

141
00:05:25,415 --> 00:05:28,205
specifically my innate curiosity.

142
00:05:28,205 --> 00:05:29,960
When I started at Google,

143
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:32,365
I didn't have a background
in software engineering,

144
00:05:32,365 --> 00:05:34,530
but I'm a naturally
curious person

145
00:05:34,530 --> 00:05:36,330
and I'm not afraid
to ask questions.

146
00:05:36,330 --> 00:05:37,715
When I join a new team,

147
00:05:37,715 --> 00:05:39,350
I find the engineer
who seems to have

148
00:05:39,350 --> 00:05:41,090
the most knowledge or experience,

149
00:05:41,090 --> 00:05:43,865
and I ask them to teach
me how everything works.

150
00:05:43,865 --> 00:05:45,380
Most people are genuinely

151
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:47,350
interested in sharing
what they know.

152
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:49,340
That initial chat usually

153
00:05:49,340 --> 00:05:51,170
leads to conversations
over lunch,

154
00:05:51,170 --> 00:05:53,060
which helps me to build
my own knowledge

155
00:05:53,060 --> 00:05:55,280
and personal history
with my colleague.

156
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:57,170
And through these
conversations, I learn

157
00:05:57,170 --> 00:05:58,985
about other teammates
to connect with,

158
00:05:58,985 --> 00:06:01,030
which increases my network.

159
00:06:01,030 --> 00:06:02,990
As you build your career,

160
00:06:02,990 --> 00:06:06,020
try to identify your
own superpowers.

161
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:08,750
Find the combination of
influencing techniques

162
00:06:08,750 --> 00:06:11,620
that best suits your
particular circumstances.

163
00:06:11,620 --> 00:06:13,880
It takes practice to
polish your skills

164
00:06:13,880 --> 00:06:15,709
and lean into various superpowers

165
00:06:15,709 --> 00:06:17,230
when working with others.

166
00:06:17,230 --> 00:06:19,570
But if you invest
time in thinking

167
00:06:19,570 --> 00:06:21,890
and troubleshooting your
influencing strategy,

168
00:06:21,890 --> 00:06:23,870
it will pay off.

169
00:06:23,870 --> 00:06:25,505
In the next video,

170
00:06:25,505 --> 00:06:28,980
we'll review and wrap
up. Meet you there.
